4. Idempotent Instantiated Typed Nodes
   1. When I run a graphfunction, its over a graph instance, the graph instance has an attached set of assets - this is a project representation
   2. But there is no reason that I can't create Project "Instances" which from the Project Graph, a new instance can be created, that instance could exist at any traversal potentially.
   3. Why is this useful,
      1. First off it makes the concept of idempotency very clear and not assumed, when i'm running a Graph Function, im passing an "instance of a project" to traverse over, or at least a fragement of the graph that Graph Function maps over.
      2. Once I understand this idempotency, i realise i can run the same graph and generate a set of A -> (B1,B2,B3,B4) each with their associated meta data traversal, fully renentrant and idempotent, by defining a new target from a common starting Node.
      3. Similarly if I want to compare two nodes directly I can use Zoom out for example and do a gap analysis betwen Requirements -> Implmentation
      4. Finally I now see the typed Graph as the minimum set of invariant assets that need to be instanced, and for which well modularised Graph Functions can be used to traverse over.
   4. Graph Functions VS Graph - to be challenged
      1. A graph is a template of a train tracks with train stations along the route you can stop at
      2. A project is an instatnce of that Graph, and as discussed above the GraphFunction engine builder can run over the same tracks repeatedly or even branch and instantiate duplicate track
